{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To determine the possible values of \\( m \\) for which the quadratic equation \\( 2x^2 + mx + 8 \\) has two distinct real roots, we need to ensure that the discriminant of the quadratic equation is positive. The general form of a quadratic equation is \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), and its discriminant is given by \\( \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\).\n\nFor the quadratic equation \\( 2x^2 + mx + 8 \\), we have \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = m \\), and \\( c = 8 \\). The discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = m^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 8 = m^2 - 64\n\\]\nFor the quadratic equation to have two distinct real roots, the discriminant must be positive:\n\\[\nm^2 - 64 > 0\n\\]\nThis inequality can be factored as:\n\\[\n(m - 8)(m + 8) > 0\n\\]\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine where the product \\((m - 8)(m + 8)\\) is positive. The critical points are \\( m = 8 \\) and \\( m = -8 \\). These points divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -8) \\), \\( (-8, 8) \\), and \\( (8, \\infty) \\).\n\n","prefix_end":948,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,8253,279,3204,2750,315,17767,296,1124,8,369,892,279,79151,23606,17767,220,17,87,61,17,488,14619,488,220,23,1124,8,702,1378,12460,1931,19703,11,582,1184,311,5978,429,279,49319,517,315,279,79151,23606,374,6785,13,576,4586,1352,315,264,79151,23606,374,17767,3859,61,17,488,44241,488,272,284,220,15,1124,701,323,1181,49319,517,374,2661,553,17767,1124,20277,284,293,61,17,481,220,19,580,1124,3593,2461,279,79151,23606,17767,220,17,87,61,17,488,14619,488,220,23,1124,701,582,614,17767,264,284,220,17,1124,701,17767,293,284,296,1124,701,323,17767,272,284,220,23,1124,568,576,49319,517,374,510,59,9640,59,20277,284,296,61,17,481,220,19,1124,50853,220,17,1124,50853,220,23,284,296,61,17,481,220,21,19,198,59,921,2461,279,79151,23606,311,614,1378,12460,1931,19703,11,279,49319,517,1969,387,