is inequality can be factored as:\n\\[\n(m - 8)(m + 8) > 0\n\\]\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine where the product \\((m - 8)(m + 8)\\) is positive. The critical points are \\( m = 8 \\) and \\( m = -8 \\). These points divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -8) \\), \\( (-8, 8) \\), and \\( (8, \\infty) \\).\n\nWe test a point in each interval:\n1. For \\( m \\in (-\\infty, -8) \\), choose \\( m = -9 \\):\n   \\[\n   (-9 - 8)(-9 + 8) = (-17)(-1) = 17 > 0\n   \\]\n2. For \\( m \\in (-8, 8) \\), choose \\( m = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   (0 - 8)(0 + 8) = (-8)(8) = -64 < 0\n   \\]\n3. For \\( m \\in (8, \\infty) \\), choose \\( m = 9 \\):\n   \\[\n   (9 - 8)(9 + 8) = (1)(17) = 17 > 0\n   \\]\n\nFrom this analysis, we see that the inequality \\( (m - 8)(m + 8) > 0 \\) holds for \\( m \\in (-\\infty, -8) \\cup (8, \\infty) \\).\n\nTherefore, the possible values of \\( m \\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, -8) \\cup (8, \\infty)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(-\\infty, -8) \\cup (8, \\infty)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:40"}
